  "title": "Simple Tenses",
  "emoji": "⏰",
  "parentNote": "Tenses tell us WHEN something happens. Use daily activities to practice: \"Yesterday I played (past). Today I play (present). Tomorrow I will play (future).\" Time words are helpful clues!",
  "learnBullets": [
    "What **tense** means — it tells us when something happens",
    "**Simple Present**: things that happen regularly or are always true",
    "**Simple Past**: things that already happened (yesterday, last week)",
    "**Simple Future**: things that will happen later (tomorrow, next week)"
  ],
  "learnItFirst": "**What is Tense?**\nTense tells us **when** an action happens — now, before, or later.\n\n**The Three Simple Tenses:**\n\n| Tense | When | Example | Time Words |\n|-------|------|---------|------------|\n| Simple Present | Now, regularly, always | I **play** every day. | always, usually, every day, sometimes |\n| Simple Past | Before, already done | I **played** yesterday. | yesterday, last week, ago, in 2020 |\n| Simple Future | Later, not yet | I **will play** tomorrow. | tomorrow, next week, soon, later |\n\n**Simple Present:**\n- For habits: She **brushes** her teeth every morning.\n- For facts: The sun **rises** in the east.\n- Add **-s** or **-es** for he/she/it: He run**s**, She go**es**\n\n**Simple Past:**\n- Add **-ed** to most verbs: play → play**ed**, walk → walk**ed**\n- Some verbs change completely: go → **went**, eat → **ate**, see → **saw**\n\n**Simple Future:**\n- Use **will + verb**: I **will** go, She **will** come\n- Use **going to**: I am **going to** play\n\n### 🧠 How to Choose the Right Tense\n1. **Look for time words:** yesterday = past, tomorrow = future\n2. **Ask:** Did it already happen? Is it happening now? Will it happen later?\n3. **Match** the verb form to the time\n\n---",
